{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ab7d9bc8142427e5e","tool_use_id":"toolu_01BubS7L1yBo55Dc95dZrrPq","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":45164,"tool_uses":6,"duration_ms":142978},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"9fce9aea-f86e-49ff-8f24-5a65bfbe2015","session_id":"6588cd15-b944-4a4a-9536-76885190680c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01558j6VCPoXxfKPrVetiVXT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V5Z6ECiDG2YMYABL3oWuDQ","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods + Experiments\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThis paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation as a new task: given a text passage, predict how many recursive fine-tuning cycles it has undergone (depth 0 through 3). The authors construct a benchmark spanning three 1-1.5B model families, three decoding strategies, and four depths, extracting 19 reference-model perplexity features and training Random Forest / Logistic Regression classifiers. A \"Depth Degradation Atlas\" maps systematic patterns across configurations (universal depth markers, non-uniform inter-depth distances, EDD boundary, strategy-dependent trajectory reversals). The paper validates signal persistence at 14B scale (~13pp attenuation), under prompted generation (~80% retention), and proposes a two-stage cross-strategy pipeline whose oracle variant recovers within-strategy performance but whose practical variant underperforms naive pooling due to poor strategy identification.\n\n## 2. Methodology Assessment\n\n### Task formalization (ordinal depth estimation)\nThe formalization in Section 3.1 is clean and well-motivated. Framing depth estimation as ordinal classification (rather than regression or binary detection) is the paper's core conceptual contribution and is well-justified by the argument that depth-0 vs. depth-1 text has fundamentally different practical implications than depth-2 vs. depth-3. The restriction to K=3 (four depths) is reasonable for a first study, though the paper does not discuss how the framework would scale to larger K.\n\n### Recursive generation protocol\nThe protocol (Section 3.2) is straightforward: LoRA fine-tuning at each depth, 5,000 samples per depth, nucleus/temperature/top-k strategies. This is sound for a controlled study. However, several methodological concerns arise:\n\n- **LoRA rank and hyperparameters are not specified.** The paper says \"using LoRA\" but never states the rank, alpha, target modules, learning rate, or number of training epochs. This is a critical omission for reproducibility, since LoRA rank directly controls the magnitude of distributional shift and thus the difficulty of the depth estimation task. A rank-4 LoRA produces very different depth signals than rank-64.\n- **Fine-tuning data is circular.** Each M_{k+1} is fine-tuned on text generated by M_k. The paper does not discuss whether the fine-tuning corpus at each depth is the same 5,000 samples used for evaluation, or whether separate train/eval splits are maintained at the generation stage. If the classifier's test samples were also used as fine-tuning data for the next depth, there is a subtle data contamination concern.\n- **Only WikiText-103 as seed corpus.** The paper mentions \"the model's original training corpus (WikiText-103 or equivalent)\" but does not clarify what \"equivalent\" means for OLMo and GPT-2 XL. Domain sensitivity of the depth signal is untested.\n\n### Feature design (19 features, Pythia-410M reference)\nThe 19 features spanning five families (perplexity statistics, surprisal statistics, distributional shape, sequence dynamics, tail statistics) are reasonably comprehensive. The motivation for each family is implicit but not explicitly argued. The choice of Pythia-410M as reference model is justified by the circularity-avoidance argument (smaller than all generators), but the paper acknowledges in Limitations that cross-reference-model robustness has not been evaluated. This is a meaningful gap: if the depth signal is reference-model-dependent, the practical utility is limited. The paper would benefit from at least one ablation with an alternative reference model (e.g., GPT-2 small).\n\n### EDD metric\nThe EDD definition (Equation 1) is clear and useful. It provides a compact summary of how many recursive depths remain distinguishable at a given threshold. The extension to EDD(M, S, T, |theta|) incorporating model scale is a nice conceptual contribution. The sensitivity analysis across four thresholds (Table 4) adds value. Minor concern: EDD is defined on pairwise AUCs of adjacent depths only, but the non-uniform distance pattern means non-adjacent pairs (e.g., d0v3) could remain discriminable even when adjacent pairs fall below threshold. The metric captures worst-case adjacent discriminability, which is appropriate but could be complemented by a \"weakest-link\" vs. \"any-pair\" distinction.\n\n### Classifiers (RF, LR)\nUsing RF and LR is appropriate for a feature-engineering study. The paper correctly positions itself as investigating what features carry depth signal, not as building a state-of-the-art classifier. However, the absence of any neural baseline (even a simple MLP on the 19 features) is a missed opportunity. More importantly, the paper does not explore whether end-to-end approaches (e.g., fine-tuning a small classifier on raw text) could capture depth signals that the 19 features miss, beyond the DCOL negative result. The DCOL experiment uses RoBERTa-large [CLS] embeddings, which is a very specific architectural choice; the negative result does not rule out all neural approaches.\n\n## 3. Experiment Assessment\n\n### Coverage vs. planned experiments\nThe review_context.yaml lists five planned experiments:\n1. **DCOL Component Ablation (plan_010)** -- cancelled (correctly, given DCOL v6 negative result). The paper reports the DCOL negative result in Section 5.5. Covered.\n2. **14B Clean FP16 Scale Validation (plan_014)** -- fully covered in Section 5.2. All key metrics (4-class accuracy, pairwise AUC, EDD, per-class metrics, feature ranking) are reported. Covered.\n3. **Wild-data Calibration on RedPajama/Dolma (plan_015)** -- deferred to AAAI. Mentioned briefly in Limitations (\"preliminary calibration experiments... suggest that false positive rates are content-quality-driven\"). Not in the paper proper. This is acceptable for scope management but leaves a gap in ecological validity.\n4. **Prompted Generation (plan_016)** -- fully covered in Section 5.3. Covered.\n5. **Cross-Strategy Two-Stage Pipeline (plan_017)** -- fully covered in Section 5.4. Covered.\n\n**Coverage assessment: 4/5 planned experiments are covered, 1 deferred. No critical gaps for the paper's claims, but the wild-data calibration would have strengthened the practical narrative.**\n\n### 1B Benchmark (Table 1)\nThe 70-85% range across the 9-cell grid is convincing, substantially above the 25% chance level. The range itself is informative: temperature sampling consistently yields higher accuracy than nucleus/top-k, which is a meaningful finding about strategy-dependent signal strength. The grid is sufficient for the paper's scope (3 models x 3 strategies). However:\n- No confidence intervals are reported for the 9 cells of Table 1. Given 5,000 samples per depth (1,000 test), CIs would be tight, but their absence is a statistical reporting gap.\n- No significance tests comparing cells. The paper describes patterns (e.g., temperature > nucleus > top-k) without testing whether differences are significant.\n- Three-class accuracy (59-82%) is mentioned in text but not tabulated, reducing verifiability.\n\n### 14B Validation (Section 5.2)\n67% with ~13pp attenuation is a reasonable finding. The paper reports CIs here (95% CI: [65.5, 68.5] for RF), which is good. The comparison uses \"1B nucleus mean\" (76%), which is fair since the 14B experiment also uses nucleus. The per-class analysis (Table 3) reveals that depth-2 is the hardest class (F1=0.46), consistent with the expected compression. The EDD analysis (Table 4) is informative.\n\nConcern: The 14B experiment uses only nucleus p=0.95, not all three strategies. While this is defensible for cost reasons, it limits the generality of the scale-attenuation claim. The paper does not explicitly acknowledge this.\n\n### Prompted Generation (Section 5.3)\n80% retention (67% vs. 83.3% BOS baseline) is a useful finding. CIs are reported ([65.4, 68.6]). The feature importance stability analysis (Table 8, weight ratios 0.78-0.85) is well-executed and supports the \"same signal, different SNR\" narrative. The experiment uses only GPT-2 XL with nucleus, limiting generality.\n\nConcern: Only 10 topic prompts are used. The paper does not analyze variance across prompts or report per-prompt accuracy. If certain prompts preserve depth signal much better than others, the aggregate 67% masks important heterogeneity. This is a minor gap but worth noting.\n\n### Cross-Strategy Pipeline (Section 5.4)\nThis is the most analytically sophisticated section. The five-condition ladder (LOO 59.1% -> Pooled 72.0% -> Predicted 70.6% -> Oracle 78.4% -> Within 78.2%) is well-structured and the gap analysis (Table 11) is informative.\n\nThe oracle-within match (0.2pp gap) is the strongest result in this section, cleanly validating the decomposition principle. The negative result (predicted < naive pooling by 1.4pp) is honestly reported and the breakeven analysis (65-70% strategy accuracy needed) adds practical value.\n\nHowever:\n- The paper does not report CIs for the cross-strategy conditions, making it impossible to assess whether the 1.4pp gap (predicted vs. naive) is statistically significant or within noise.\n- The LOO baseline (59.1%) is described as a mean but individual LOO conditions are not broken out. The temperature-holdout scenario is mentioned as catastrophic (d1v2 AUC as low as 0.13) but not fully tabulated.\n\n### DCOL Negative Result (Section 5.5)\nThis section is informative, not filler. The negative result (64.5% vs. 78.2% for RF, with severe overfitting) provides a useful boundary condition: semantic text representations do not capture distributional depth signals. The mechanistic explanation (RoBERTa [CLS] encodes content, not distributional shift) is well-reasoned. The section is appropriately concise (~1/3 page).\n\nHowever, as noted above, DCOL with RoBERTa [CLS] is one specific neural approach. The paper slightly overgeneralizes by implying that contrastive ordinal learning on text depth estimation has \"inherent limitations\" when the limitation may be specific to the representation choice.\n\n### Statistical rigor\n- **CIs**: Reported for 14B (Table 2 area) and prompted generation (Table 6 area), but missing for the core 1B benchmark (Table 1) and cross-strategy conditions (Table 10).\n- **Sample sizes**: 5,000 per depth, 80/20 split = 1,000 test samples per class. Adequate for the reported precision.\n- **Significance tests**: No formal significance tests anywhere. Differences between models, strategies, and conditions are discussed as patterns without statistical testing.\n- **Multiple comparisons**: Not addressed. With 9 cells in Table 1 and multiple pairwise AUCs, some correction would strengthen the analysis.\n\n### Reproducibility\nCritical gaps:\n- LoRA hyperparameters (rank, alpha, learning rate, epochs, target modules) are not specified.\n- Text generation parameters beyond strategy type (e.g., max length, repetition penalty) are not specified.\n- The truncation length for feature extraction is not mentioned in the paper (the review_context mentions 498 tokens, but this does not appear in the paper text).\n- Whether the same random seed is used across configurations is not discussed.\n- Code/data availability is not mentioned.\n\n## 4. Strengths\n\n1. **Well-defined new task.** Ordinal depth estimation is a genuinely novel and well-motivated problem formulation that advances beyond binary synthetic text detection. The practical motivation (graduated data curation) is compelling.\n\n2. **Systematic benchmark design.** The 3-model x 3-strategy grid at 1B scale provides comprehensive coverage. The Depth Degradation Atlas organizes findings along four interpretable dimensions.\n\n3. **Honest reporting of negative and attenuated results.** The paper does not oversell. Scale attenuation (~13pp), prompted generation attenuation (~16pp), the predicted two-stage pipeline underperforming naive pooling, and the DCOL failure are all reported transparently with quantified gaps.\n\n4. **Strong analytical framework for cross-strategy deployment.** The five-condition accuracy ladder with oracle/predicted decomposition is methodologically clean. The oracle-within match (0.2pp) is a convincing validation of the decomposition principle.\n\n5. **EDD as a compact summary metric.** The Effective Discriminable Depth concept and its scale-dependent extension provide a useful tool for characterizing depth signal strength across configurations.\n\n6. **Feature universality finding.** The consistent appearance of p90_ppl and var_surprisal in top-5 across all configurations, with interpretable rank shifts at scale, is a robust empirical finding.\n\n## 5. Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Missing LoRA and generation hyperparameters.** The entire experimental framework depends on LoRA fine-tuning producing measurable distributional shifts, but no LoRA hyperparameters are reported. Different LoRA configurations could produce trivially easy or impossibly hard depth estimation tasks. This fundamentally undermines reproducibility and makes it impossible to assess whether the reported accuracies are inherent properties of the task or artifacts of a specific fine-tuning configuration.\n\n2. **(Major) No sensitivity analysis to fine-tuning intensity.** The depth signal is a direct consequence of how much each LoRA fine-tuning step changes the model distribution. The paper treats the fine-tuning configuration as fixed and reports depth estimation accuracy as if it were an intrinsic property, but accuracy would change dramatically with different LoRA ranks, learning rates, or training durations. At minimum, the paper should include an ablation showing how depth estimation accuracy varies with fine-tuning intensity.\n\n3. **(Major) Incomplete statistical reporting.** The core 1B benchmark (Table 1) lacks confidence intervals, and no significance tests are reported anywhere. For a paper whose central claims rest on comparing accuracy numbers across conditions (e.g., temperature > nucleus, 1B > 14B), the absence of formal statistical testing is a significant gap.\n\n4. **(Minor) Single reference model without robustness check.** All 19 features depend on Pythia-410M. While the circularity argument justifies choosing a model smaller than the generators, the paper does not test whether a different reference model (e.g., GPT-2 small, OLMo-350M) would produce similar features and accuracies. This limits confidence in the generality of findings.\n\n5. **(Minor) Limited ecological validity of prompted generation.** Only 10 topic prompts are used with a single model (GPT-2 XL). No per-prompt breakdown is provided. Real-world prompted generation involves far more diverse and complex prompts (instruction-following, dialogue, etc.), making the 80% retention claim hard to extrapolate.\n\n6. **(Minor) Narrow model family coverage.** All three 1B models are autoregressive English-language models from roughly the same era. The 14B model (Qwen2.5-14B) is the only non-Western model family. The paper does not test instruction-tuned models, chat models, or models from different training paradigms (e.g., mixture-of-experts). The \"recursive generation\" in practice often involves instruction-tuned models generating training data.\n\n7. **(Minor) DCOL negative result is underexplored.** The paper concludes that contrastive ordinal learning has \"inherent limitations\" for depth estimation, but only tests one representation (RoBERTa-large [CLS]). Token-level representations, perplexity-based embeddings, or multi-scale representations might perform differently. The claim is stronger than the evidence supports.\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. What are the LoRA hyperparameters (rank, alpha, target modules, learning rate, epochs) used for recursive fine-tuning? How sensitive are the depth estimation accuracies to these choices?\n\n2. How is the training/evaluation split handled at the generation stage? Are the 5,000 passages used for classifier evaluation the same ones used to fine-tune the next-depth model?\n\n3. What is the truncation length for feature extraction? The paper does not mention this, but it could significantly affect perplexity-based features.\n\n4. Have you tested any alternative reference models beyond Pythia-410M? Even a single additional reference model would substantially strengthen the generality claim.\n\n5. For the prompted generation experiment, what is the variance in depth estimation accuracy across the 10 topic prompts? Are there prompts that preserve the signal much better or worse than others?\n\n6. The paper claims ~13pp attenuation from 1B to 14B. Can you provide a more principled comparison? The 1B \"nucleus mean\" averages three different model architectures, while the 14B result is a single model (Qwen). Architecture differences may confound the scale comparison.\n\n7. For the cross-strategy pipeline, is the 1.4pp gap (predicted vs. naive) statistically significant? Without CIs on these conditions, it is unclear whether this is a real effect or noise.\n\n8. Why not test a simple MLP on the 19 features as a third classifier? This would bridge the gap between RF/LR and the DCOL neural approach.\n\n## 7. Scores\n\n- **Soundness**: 3.0 -- The main claims are supported by the experiments, but the missing fine-tuning hyperparameters and sensitivity analysis create a gap between what is claimed (depth estimation is feasible as a general property of recursive generation) and what is shown (depth estimation works under one specific, unspecified fine-tuning configuration). Statistical reporting is incomplete.\n\n- **Excitement**: 3.5 -- The problem formulation is genuinely novel and the practical motivation is timely. The Depth Degradation Atlas and EDD concept are useful contributions. However, the practical impact is limited by the controlled-setting-only evaluation and the cross-strategy deployment bottleneck.\n\n- **Overall**: 3.0 -- Findings-level paper. The task formalization and systematic empirical study are valuable contributions to the community, and the honest reporting of limitations is appreciated. However, the reproducibility concerns (missing hyperparameters), incomplete statistical rigor, and narrow ecological validity prevent conference-level acceptance. Addressing the LoRA sensitivity analysis and statistical testing would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n- **Confidence**: 4 -- I am quite sure of my assessment. I have carefully read the paper and cross-referenced with the experiment design. My expertise covers statistical text analysis and machine learning evaluation methodology.\n","project":"recursive_gen_depth_est","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260517T075852","overall_score":"borderline reject / findings accept"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":699,"cache_read_input_tokens":44442,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":699,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BubS7L1yBo55Dc95dZrrPq","session_id":"6588cd15-b944-4a4a-9536-76885190680c","uuid":"8016eb04-5b9b-4f8d-99aa-30a6ee0323dd"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V5Z6ECiDG2YMYABL3oWuDQ","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"report_path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513_2/projects/recursive_gen_depth_est/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T075852/median/review_report.md\\\", \\\"dream_id\\\": \\\"dream_20260517T075852\\\", \\\"tier\\\": \\\"median\\\", \\\"review_visible\\\": true}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BubS7L1yBo55Dc95dZrrPq","session_id":"6588cd15-b944-4a4a-9536-76885190680c","uuid":"508b1081-e094-42c1-844a-d8b86a53a038","timestamp":"2026-05-17T08:53:55.448Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a636f1ca35a0aace8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rqg17FH3ToWGz7ocFeN6xk","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":45137,"tool_uses":6,"duration_ms":140502},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"08663493-c39d-4005-becf-9857ee7b557a","session_id":"6588cd15-b944-4a4a-9536-76885190680c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014Yyg3JGbgnCeGGAy7beiBx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Rm9XnMNMQwS8VuL27B47Qh","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Review Report: R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n## Paper: \"How Deep Is This Text? Ordinal Depth Estimation for Recursively Generated Content\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThis paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation as a new task: given a text passage, predict how many recursive fine-tuning cycles it has undergone (depth 0 through 3). The authors construct a benchmark spanning three 1-1.5B model families, three decoding strategies, and four depths, using 19 reference-model perplexity features with Random Forest and Logistic Regression classifiers. They present a Depth Degradation Atlas documenting how statistical properties evolve across depths, validate the signal at 14B scale and under prompted generation, and propose a two-stage decomposition for cross-strategy deployment. Within-strategy RF accuracy ranges from 70-85% at 1B scale, with quantified attenuation at 14B (67%, approximately -13pp) and under prompted generation (67%, 80% retention).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Novelty Assessment\n\n**Task formalization (ordinal vs. binary).** The shift from binary \"real vs. synthetic\" to ordinal depth estimation is a genuinely useful reframing. The motivation is well-articulated: first-generation synthetic text may be harmless or even beneficial, while deeply recursive text drives distributional collapse. This is the paper's strongest conceptual contribution. However, the novelty is partially tempered by the fact that the task definition itself is straightforward once one recognizes that model collapse studies already track depth as a variable. The key insight is treating depth as an *unknown* to be *estimated* rather than a *known* experimental condition, which is a meaningful but not dramatic conceptual leap.\n\n**Depth Degradation Atlas.** The Atlas organizes observations about feature evolution across depths, strategies, and scales into a structured map. While the individual observations (perplexity increases with depth, tail statistics are informative, features converge toward an attractor) are largely predictable from model collapse theory, the systematic cataloguing across 9 configurations plus scale/mode extensions has descriptive value. The Atlas is better characterized as a thorough empirical characterization than a conceptual breakthrough. It risks being seen as \"a collection of expected observations presented with a catchy name.\"\n\n**EDD concept.** Effective Discriminable Depth is essentially a threshold-based summary statistic on pairwise AUC values. The formulation (Equation 1) is clean and useful for comparing configurations, but it is definitionally simple. The extension to scale-dependent EDD (adding |theta| as a parameter) is noted but not deeply explored beyond the single 14B data point. As a concept, EDD is more of a convenient reporting metric than a theoretical contribution.\n\n**Scale-dependent attenuation.** The finding that 14B models show compressed depth signals (-13pp, EDD drops from 3-4 to 2) is informative and practically important. However, with only one data point at 14B (single model, single strategy), the \"scale-dependent\" claim is based on a binary comparison (1B vs. 14B) rather than a true scaling analysis. The mechanistic explanation (larger parameter counts dilute fixed-rank LoRA perturbations) is reasonable but not empirically validated beyond correlation.\n\n**Strategy-dependent trajectory reversal.** The temperature sampling reversal finding (d1v2 AUC as low as 0.13 for temperature holdout) is the most surprising empirical result. The mechanistic explanation (uniform logit scaling vs. truncation-based methods) is intuitive and well-argued. This is a genuine contribution to understanding decoding strategy interactions with recursive generation.\n\n**Two-stage decomposition.** The oracle-vs-within analysis (78.4% vs. 78.2%) cleanly validates the decomposition principle. The finding that the practical bottleneck is strategy identification (57.5%) rather than depth classification is useful. However, the analysis approach (oracle upper bound, naive pooling baseline, predicted pipeline) follows standard machine learning methodology and is not methodologically novel.\n\n**Overall novelty verdict.** The paper's novelty is moderate. The task formalization is the primary conceptual contribution, supported by thorough empirical work. The findings are more characterization-oriented than insight-generating. The paper advances understanding incrementally rather than providing a fundamentally new perspective or method.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Related Work Assessment\n\n**Model collapse literature.** Adequately covered. Shumailov et al. (2024), Alemohammad et al. (2024), Dohmatob et al. (2024), Gerstgrasser et al. (2024), and Drayson et al. (2025) form the core. The positioning (\"these works characterize *what happens* but assume depth is known; we ask whether depth can be *estimated*\") is clear and honest.\n\n**Missing from model collapse:** The paper omits several relevant works:\n- Briesch et al. (2023) on iterative retraining dynamics in smaller generative models.\n- Hataya et al. (2023) on self-consuming generative models beyond text (vision domain, but the theoretical framework is relevant).\n- Bertrand et al. (2024) on stability conditions for iterative training, which directly relates to the attractor convergence discussion.\n\n**Synthetic text detection.** The coverage spans watermarking (Kirchenbauer et al., 2023), statistical methods (Hans et al., 2024; Bao et al., 2024), and learned classifiers (Uchendu et al., 2020). However, several important methods are missing:\n- DetectGPT (Mitchell et al., 2023) is cited in the references but barely discussed in the Related Work section itself (it appears only in the reference list).\n- GPTZero and commercial detection tools are not discussed, even to note why they are excluded.\n- DNA-GPT (Yang et al., 2023) and other perturbation-based methods.\n- The PHD (Tulchinskii et al., 2024) intrinsic dimensionality approach, which uses geometric properties of text representations and would be a natural comparison point for the feature-based approach here.\n- Revision detection / multi-pass generation detection literature is not discussed, though it is conceptually adjacent.\n\n**Data quality and curation.** Minimal coverage (Xie et al., 2023; Wettig et al., 2024 only). This section would benefit from mentioning FineWeb (Penedo et al., 2024, which is cited in references but not discussed in Related Work), DSIR, and other data selection methods that could serve as downstream consumers of depth estimates.\n\n**Decoding strategy effects.** Adequate but thin. Holtzman et al. (2020) and Garces Arias et al. (2025) are the only citations. The literature on how sampling parameters affect text quality and detectability is larger. Notably missing: Pillutla et al. (2021) on MAUVE, which provides distributional comparison metrics relevant to understanding depth-dependent shifts; and the broader literature on sampling temperature effects on LLM outputs.\n\n**Positioning against prior work.** Generally honest. The authors do not overclaim novelty. The framing \"no existing method estimates ordinal generation depth from text alone\" appears accurate based on my knowledge. The paper correctly notes that binary detector confidence scores \"correlate weakly with generation count but are not calibrated for ordinal estimation.\"\n\n**Work that could undermine claims.** I am not aware of published work that directly estimates recursive generation depth. However, the strong performance of simple perplexity features raises the question of whether existing binary detectors could be trivially recalibrated for ordinal estimation (e.g., by binning confidence scores). The paper mentions this possibility but dismisses it in one sentence without empirical comparison. A calibration experiment with Binoculars or Fast-DetectGPT scores would strengthen or weaken the claimed novelty.\n\n**Reference count.** 22 references total. This is on the low side for an EMNLP Main submission. A typical EMNLP paper in this area would cite 35-50 references. The Related Work section feels compressed.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Writing Quality\n\n**Narrative coherence.** The narrative from Abstract to Conclusion is coherent and well-structured. The story \"task formalization -> Atlas -> robustness -> deployment\" flows logically. The paper maintains a consistent thread: depth estimation is feasible, the signal has specific properties, and practical deployment faces identified challenges.\n\n**Claim calibration.** Generally well-calibrated. The authors are commendably honest about limitations:\n- The 14B attenuation is reported as \"signal persists but with reduced discriminability\" rather than \"robust across scales.\"\n- The two-stage pipeline is framed as \"validated principle, identified bottleneck\" rather than a solved problem.\n- The prompted generation result is presented as \"80% retention\" with explicit acknowledgment of the 20% gap.\n- The DCOL negative result is included transparently.\n\nOne area of mild overclaiming: calling the collection of observations a \"Depth Degradation Atlas\" and listing it as a separate contribution gives it more conceptual weight than warranted. It is a systematic empirical study, not a conceptual framework.\n\n**AI-writing artifacts.** The paper is generally clean of obvious AI-writing markers. I did not detect excessive em-dashes, -ing phrase endings, or formulaic transitions. The prose is functional academic English. Minor note: some paragraph transitions are slightly mechanical (\"A natural concern is whether...\"; \"To address the concern that...\"), but these are within normal academic writing conventions.\n\n**Structure.** Well-structured for a 10-page submission. The section organization (Task/Method -> Atlas -> Experiments -> Discussion -> Limitations -> Conclusion) is standard and effective. Tables are informative and well-formatted. The paper would benefit from at least one figure (e.g., AUC decay curves across depths, or a visual Atlas summary), as the paper is entirely text and tables. The absence of any figures is unusual and makes the paper harder to quickly parse.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Strengths\n\n1. **Well-motivated task formalization.** The shift from binary detection to ordinal depth estimation is practically relevant and fills a genuine gap. The motivation through data curation applications is convincing.\n\n2. **Systematic benchmark design.** Three model families, three decoding strategies, four depths, 5,000 samples per condition, with consistent feature extraction. The experimental design is thorough and enables meaningful comparisons.\n\n3. **Honest reporting of negative and attenuated results.** The 14B attenuation (-13pp), prompted generation gap (-16pp), DCOL failure, and two-stage pipeline underperformance are all reported transparently. This builds trust in the positive findings.\n\n4. **Strategy-dependent trajectory reversal.** The finding that temperature sampling produces qualitatively different degradation trajectories, with mechanistic explanation, is the most novel empirical contribution and has implications for both detection and collapse research.\n\n5. **Clean two-stage decomposition analysis.** The oracle-vs-within match (0.2pp gap) cleanly isolates strategy mismatch as the sole source of cross-strategy degradation. The breakeven analysis (65-70% strategy accuracy needed) provides actionable guidance.\n\n6. **Thorough limitations section.** Five specific limitations are identified with concrete descriptions, not hand-waving.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Limited model scale coverage.** The core benchmark uses only 1-1.5B models. The single 14B experiment (one model, one strategy) is insufficient to characterize \"scale dependence.\" Claims about scale-dependent EDD and signal attenuation rest on a binary comparison. Testing at 7B and 70B+ would substantially strengthen the contribution. The extrapolation that the signal \"may further compress\" at 70B+ is speculative.\n\n2. **(Major) No comparison with existing detection methods repurposed for ordinal estimation.** The paper claims existing detectors cannot estimate depth, but does not empirically test whether confidence scores from Binoculars, Fast-DetectGPT, or DetectGPT could be calibrated for ordinal prediction. This is the most obvious baseline and its absence weakens the novelty claim. Even a simple experiment binning these scores into ordinal categories would be informative.\n\n3. **(Major) No figures in the entire paper.** A paper proposing a \"Depth Degradation Atlas\" and analyzing feature trajectories across depths contains zero figures. This is a significant presentation weakness. AUC decay curves, feature trajectory plots, confusion matrices, or an Atlas heatmap would dramatically improve readability and impact.\n\n4. **(Minor) Feature engineering is straightforward.** The 19 features are standard statistical summaries of perplexity distributions. While they work, the feature design does not constitute a methodological contribution. The paper does not explore learned features, neural classifiers beyond DCOL (which failed for arguably fixable reasons), or representation learning approaches.\n\n5. **(Minor) Narrow generation protocol.** All recursive chains use LoRA fine-tuning with fixed hyperparameters. Real-world recursive generation involves full fine-tuning, RLHF, different data mixing ratios, and heterogeneous model families across generations. The ecological validity argument from prompted generation is only partial.\n\n6. **(Minor) Reference model dependency is not explored.** All experiments use Pythia-410M as the reference model. The paper acknowledges this dependency but does not test sensitivity to reference model choice. Since the entire feature set depends on this model, robustness to reference model selection is important.\n\n7. **(Minor) Thin Related Work section.** 22 references is below typical EMNLP density. Key detection methods (DetectGPT discussion, PHD, perturbation-based approaches) and recent model collapse theory papers are missing. The data curation subsection has only two citations.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you tested whether confidence scores from Binoculars or Fast-DetectGPT, when treated as continuous predictors for ordinal regression, can achieve comparable depth estimation accuracy? This would clarify whether the novelty lies in the task formalization or whether existing tools already implicitly solve it.\n\n2. The DCOL failure is attributed to RoBERTa-large encoding semantic content rather than distributional depth signal. Have you considered using the reference-model perplexity features as input to an ordinal neural classifier (e.g., a simple MLP with ordinal loss), rather than raw text? This would test whether the ordinal learning framework adds value on top of the already-effective features.\n\n3. For the 14B experiment, why was only nucleus sampling tested? Given that temperature sampling produces the most distinctive behavior at 1B scale, testing temperature at 14B would reveal whether the trajectory reversal persists at scale.\n\n4. The paper mentions \"preliminary calibration experiments on RedPajama and Dolma\" in the Limitations section. Can you share these results, even briefly? They would address the wild-type generalization concern.\n\n5. How sensitive are the results to the number of recursive steps in the LoRA fine-tuning at each depth? If the fine-tuning duration changes, does the depth signal shift proportionally, or is it robust to training hyperparameters?\n\n6. Why are there no figures in the paper? The Depth Degradation Atlas concept would benefit enormously from visual representation, and AUC decay curves would make the scale-dependent attenuation finding immediately intuitive.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Scores\n\n- **Soundness**: 3.5 — The main claims are supported by the experiments presented, but the absence of comparison with repurposed existing detectors and the single-point scale analysis leave gaps in the evidence base.\n\n- **Excitement**: 3.0 — The task formalization is interesting and the empirical work is thorough, but the findings are largely characterization of expected phenomena (perplexity increases with recursive depth) rather than surprising insights. The strategy-dependent reversal is the most exciting finding. Overall, this is a solid empirical contribution but unlikely to generate significant follow-up work.\n\n- **Overall**: 3.0 — This paper is in the Findings-level range. The task formalization has value, the experiments are competently executed, and the reporting is honest. However, the novelty is incremental (ordinal reframing of a known problem), the methods are standard (RF on hand-crafted features), and the most impactful findings (scale attenuation, strategy reversal) are under-explored. For EMNLP Main, I would expect either a stronger methodological contribution or more surprising empirical findings.\n\n- **Confidence**: 4 — I am quite familiar with synthetic text detection and model collapse literature. I have carefully read the full paper and experiment design reference.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Assessment\n\nThis is a competent empirical paper that formalizes an interesting task (ordinal depth estimation) and executes a systematic benchmark study. The strengths lie in the thorough experimental design, honest reporting, and the strategy-reversal finding. The weaknesses are the absence of comparison with repurposed existing detectors (the most critical gap), limited scale coverage, no figures, and thin Related Work. The paper makes a modest contribution suitable for Findings but falls short of the novelty and impact threshold for EMNLP Main. The core risk is that the task, while well-defined, may be solvable by trivial extensions of existing detection methods, and the paper does not rule this out.\n","project":"recursive_gen_depth_est","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260517T075852","overall_score":"borderline reject / Findings accept"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":683,"cache_read_input_tokens":44427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":683,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rqg17FH3ToWGz7ocFeN6xk","session_id":"6588cd15-b944-4a4a-9536-76885190680c","uuid":"faf62700-a033-4e9c-86fc-4021e1bc6a6c"}
